## Why This Is Asked

This question probes your ability to craft end-to-end tests for multi-tenant APIs under live deployments, focusing on isolation, authorization refresh behavior, streaming reliability, and per-tenant resource controls.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation across REST and WebSocket paths
- Token revocation propagation and caching implications
- Streaming data integrity: ordering, deduplication, replay safety
- Rolling deploys, canary testing, and feature flags for auth
- Per-tenant quotas and fault-injection strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test scaffold (pseudo)
import { login, fetchWithToken, wsConnect } from './test-utils'

test('tenant isolation during canary rollout', async () => {
  const tokenA = await login('tenantA')
  const tokenB = await login('tenantB')

  // REST: tenantA can access its data; tenantA cannot access tenantB data
  const r1 = await fetchWithToken('/api/tenantA/data', tokenA)
  const r2 = await fetchWithToken('/api/tenantB/data', tokenA)
  expect(r1.ok).toBe(true)
  expect(r2.status).toBe(403)

  // WebSocket: subscribe as tenantA and ensure messages belong to tenantA
  const wsA = await wsConnect('/ws/stream', tokenA)
  const msgA = await wsA.next()
  expect(msgA.tenant).toBe('tenantA')
  wsA.close()

  // Revocation: revoke tokenA and verify access denied
  await revokeToken(tokenA)
  const r3 = await fetchWithToken('/api/tenantA/data', tokenA)
  expect(r3.status).toBe(401)
})
```

